<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rostom of Kartli</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1565–<a href="/years/1658">1658</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Khosrow Mirza</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rostom, born Khosrow Mirza, was king of Kartli in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> from 1633 to 1658. A Muslim member of the Bagrationi royal house raised at the Safavid court, he was installed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safi-of-persia">Shah Safi</a> and ruled as a loyal Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, bringing Persian customs to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> while protecting the Georgian Church. His long reign gave Kartli relative peace after decades of war.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rostom-of-kartli.jpg" alt="Rostom of Kartli" width="320" height="531" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Teramo Castelli · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rostom.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rostom was born around 1565 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, the illegitimate son of David XI (Daud Khan), who governed part of Kartli on behalf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, and a concubine reportedly from the village of Chindisi; he was raised in the Islamic faith. Taken to the court of Mohammad Khodabanda in 1579, he later fell into poverty and, by some accounts, lived as a beggar in Isfahan. In 1612 the Georgian general Giorgi Saakadze befriended him and raised him out of destitution.</p><p>Appointed darugha (prefect) of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> in 1618, a post he kept for life, he became one of the closest advisers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Abbas I</a>. In January 1629 the dying shah named him qollar-aghasi, commander of the imperial guard, renamed him Rostom Khan after the hero of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a> and charged him with securing the succession of his grandson Sam Mirza, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safi-of-persia">Shah Safi</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Already 67 when he took the throne, he governed Kartli on the Safavid model, giving the royal offices Safavid titles and appointing both Persian and Georgian officials. He rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> and Gori, where he turned the citadel into a palace, built bridges, roads, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bazaar">bazaars</a>, and in 1639 abolished taxes on Georgian merchants. In 1633 he allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levan-ii-dadiani">Levan II Dadiani</a> of Mingrelia, whose sister Mariam he married.</p><p>His pro-Persian stance provoked noble revolts and plots, including a conspiracy formed in late 1639 to restore Teimuraz I, which ended with the king's victory in 1642 and the execution of Catholicos Eudemus. In 1648 he defeated Teimuraz again and annexed Kakheti. He paid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> tribute and sent troops to its wars but enjoyed wide autonomy at home. He signed decrees invoking both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> and Jesus Christ; Christians were not persecuted, though Persian music and Islamic dress spread in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Rostom died on 17 November 1658, aged 93. Having no children of his own, he adopted Prince Luarsab in 1641 and, after Luarsab's death, Vakhtang of Mukhrani, who succeeded him as Vakhtang V. David Marshall Lang counted him among the few Georgian monarchs who preferred diplomacy and stability, while Nikoloz Berdzenishvili called him a renegade and a traitor who sacrificed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>'s independence for Persian financial support.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rostom of Kartli” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kartli lay on the front line between the Ottoman and Safavid empires and was usually a Safavid vassal; several kings, such as <b>Rostom</b>, converted to Islam to hold the throne.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kartli">Kingdom of Kartli</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in Qom, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.65,51.68&amp;year=1625" aria-label="Isfahan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5876 977 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Rostom of Kartli"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6326 1247h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6326 1247h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.65,51.68&amp;year=1625">What was here in 1625? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
